The process by which nodes of the Bitcoin network connect to other nodes in order to obtain information. When a Bitcoin node is launched for the first time, it has no information about the other nodes on the network. Yet it must establish connections in order to synchronize on the blockchain with the most accumulated work. Several mechanisms are used to discover these peers, in order of priority:
* The node starts by consulting its local `peers.dat` file, which stores information about the nodes it has previously interacted with. If the node is new, this file will be empty, and the process moves on to the next step;
* In the absence of information in the `peers.dat` file (which is normal for a newly launched node), the node makes DNS queries to the DNS seeds. These servers provide a list of IP addresses of nodes presumed to be active, to establish connections with. The addresses of the DNS seeds are hard-coded in the Bitcoin Core code. This step is generally enough to complete peer discovery;
* If the DNS seeds do not respond within 60 seconds, the node can then turn to the seed nodes. These are public Bitcoin nodes listed in a static list of nearly a thousand entries built directly into the Bitcoin Core source code. The new node will use this list to establish a first connection to the network and obtain IP addresses of other nodes;
* In the very unlikely case where all the previous methods fail, the node operator can always manually add node IP addresses to establish a first connection.

Acronym for “*Partially Signed Bitcoin Transaction*”. It is a specification introduced with BIP-0174 to standardize the way unfinalized transactions are constructed in Bitcoin-related software, such as wallet software. A PSBT encapsulates a transaction in which the inputs may not be fully signed. It includes all the information necessary for a participant to be able to sign the transaction without requiring additional data. Thus, a PSBT can take 3 different forms:
* A fully constructed transaction, but not yet signed;
* A partially signed transaction, where some inputs are signed while others are not yet;
* Or a fully signed Bitcoin transaction, which can be converted to be ready for broadcast on the network.

The PSBT format facilitates interoperability between different wallet software and signing devices (hardware wallets). Currently, version 0 of PSBTs is used, as defined in BIP-0174, and version 2 was defined in BIP-0370.

*PSBT version 1 does not exist. Since version 0 was the first version, the second version was colloquially called version 2. Ava Chow, who wrote BIP-0370, therefore decided to give the number 2 to this new version in order to avoid any confusion.*

The set of all the rules observed by the software of a Bitcoin node in its interactions with the peer-to-peer network. Relay policy is the most encompassing term for designating a node's local rules, as opposed to consensus rules, which are universal.

Relay policy breaks down into increasingly specific subsets:
* The *transaction relay policy* concerns specifically the rules related to the handling of transactions on the network;
* The *mempool policy* groups together the rules for accepting transactions into the node's mempool;
* The *standardness rules* dictate the format of the individual transactions accepted.

Unlike consensus rules, relay policy is configured locally by each node and may vary from one to another.
